A||||8||Bruce||Radio
Z||000000||Bruce||06-22-99||10:53 AM||||The am and fm on my car radio don't hold the stations and pick up alot of static. When I purchased the car, the previous owner had installed metal sleeves around the plug wires that meet the spark plugs. Is there any relation to the two, regarding electrical interference, or do I just need to get the radio checked out?
Z||000001||86_Fastback||06-22-99||11:13 AM||||Bruce,<br>I believe the metal sleeves you are talking about came from factory (I have them also).  Their purpose, if I am not mistaken, is to protect the spark plug wire boot from the heat.  <p>It sounds like a bad ground or battery connection.  Verify you have a good connection to geound and then check to make sure the radio is getting a supply voltage with ignition off(so it can save memory settings).  If these are fine, my guess is the radio.<p>If it turns out to be the radio, I have a stock am/fm cassete, eq, all push button that I could sell you cheap.  It works fine, I just have everything on cd and not tape.<br>-Gregg
Z||000002||theogre||06-22-99||11:33 AM||||check All the fuses. the radio is connected to more than one.
Z||000003||Bruce||06-22-99||07:21 PM||||Thanks, guys.<br>When I bought the guy, one of the first things I did was have the plugs and wires replaced. I didn't put the sleeves back on because the mechanic said he needed to fiddle with the wires to make them fit. Should I have them put back on?
Z||000004||Cooter||06-22-99||07:37 PM||||Bruce, if the radio wont keep the presets and the clock loses time when you turn off the switch, check all fuses first, then if that doesnt do it, pull the radio and check the connector with the orange and black wires. The orange one should be hot at all times. If not, you may have a broken wire between the fuse block and the radio. Check to see if you cig. lighter is getting power, I think they are on the same circuit. You may need to look in the manual for a wiring diagram and trace the wires to find the problem.
Z||000005||Cooter||06-22-99||07:40 PM||||DUH- one more thing, check for proper grounds, especially at the antennae. A loose ground here will really cause problems with reception
Z||000006||batboy||06-22-99||07:43 PM||||Did you install resister spark plugs? Those are the type that help cut down on radio interference. Interference from the ignition usually is identified by a humming or buzzing noise that increases in pitch with engine rpm. If none of the other guys suggestions work, make sure the antenna plug is not corroded and that it is firmly seated into the radio. The antenna mask may have a separate ground too. Man, ya gotta have some tunes in that ride!
Z||000007||Bruce||06-22-99||08:19 PM||||Yes, they're resistor plugs. The tape player works great, so it has to be one of the places you guys suggested. <br>Incidentally, what about the importance of the metal sleeves for the spark plugs?<br>Thanks
Z||000008||Bozone||06-23-99||11:56 AM||||Hey 86_Fastback - if you want to sell that radio, I need one that plays cassettes. One of my GTs has the plain one. If you still have it, let me know how much. Email bozone@intap.net or reply here.
